.TH CLADENC-START 1 "" cladenc "cladenc"
.SH NAME
cladenc\-start \- start a new directed evolution and print the directory of its best harness
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBcladenc start\fP
    \fB\-\-benchmark\fP <DIR>...
    \fB\-\-corroboration\-tasks\-percentage\fP <NUMBER>
    [\fB\-\-diversification\-docker\-image\fP <IMAGE>]
    [\fB\-\-diversification\-docker\-image\-build\-timeout\fP <SECONDS>]
    [\fB\-\-diversification\-dockerfile\fP <FILE>]
    \fB\-\-diversification\-timeout\fP <SECONDS>
    [\fB\-\-harness\-build\-command\fP <COMMAND>]
    \fB\-\-harness\-run\-command\fP <COMMAND>
    [\fB\-\-help\fP]
    \fB\-\-max\-concurrent\-sandboxes\fP <COUNT>
    \fB\-\-max\-diversifications\fP <COUNT>
    \fB\-\-max\-task\-health\-check\-retries\fP <COUNT>
    \fB\-\-max\-verifier\-health\-check\-retries\fP <COUNT>
    \fB\-\-model\fP <NAME>
    [\fB\-\-model\-key\-file\fP <FILE>]
    \fB\-\-model\-url\fP <URL>
    \fB\-\-outputs\-dir\fP <DIR>
    \fB\-\-primogenitor\fP <DIR>
    \fB\-\-selection\-tasks\-percentage\fP <NUMBER>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBcladenc start\fP starts a new directed evolution, carries it to its end,
and prints the directory of the best harness on standard output, and nothing
else there.
It says on standard error what it is doing, and when the directed evolution
ends it prints there what \fBcladenc report\fP prints.
.PP
Every harness and every verifier runs in a sandbox, which is Harbor's word for
the isolated place a task is run in, and every sandbox of cladenc is a Docker
container.
.PP
Ctrl-C, or one of the signals SIGINT, SIGTERM, SIGQUIT and SIGHUP, stops it
sooner: it removes the sandboxes it has running, prints no report and exits
with status 1, and \fBcladenc resume\fP continues the directed evolution from
there.
While the directed evolution has not ended, \fBcladenc status\fP prints its
state so far.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
\fB\-\-benchmark\fP <DIR>
The directory of a benchmark, which holds a directory for each task, given
once for each benchmark.
.TP
\fB\-\-corroboration\-tasks\-percentage\fP <NUMBER>
The percentage of each benchmark's tasks that are corroboration tasks, above 0
and below 100.
.TP
\fB\-\-diversification\-docker\-image\fP <IMAGE>
The Docker image every diversification and every build of a harness is
carried out in, where \fB\-\-diversification\-dockerfile\fP is not given.
.TP
\fB\-\-diversification\-docker\-image\-build\-timeout\fP <SECONDS>
The time limit of the build of the image, given with
\fB\-\-diversification\-dockerfile\fP.
.TP
\fB\-\-diversification\-dockerfile\fP <FILE>
The Dockerfile cladenc builds the image of every diversification and every
build of a harness from, in place of
\fB\-\-diversification\-docker\-image\fP.
.TP
\fB\-\-diversification\-timeout\fP <SECONDS>
The time limit of one diversification.
.TP
\fB\-\-harness\-build\-command\fP <COMMAND>
The shell command that builds a harness in a sandbox, where one has to be
built.
.TP
\fB\-\-harness\-run\-command\fP <COMMAND>
The shell command that runs a harness in a sandbox.
.TP
\fB\-h\fP, \fB\-\-help\fP
Print help.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-concurrent\-sandboxes\fP <COUNT>
The most sandboxes running at once.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-diversifications\fP <COUNT>
The most diversifications to start.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-task\-health\-check\-retries\fP <COUNT>
The most times the sandbox of a task is brought up again, where its health
check does not pass, 0 or more.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-verifier\-health\-check\-retries\fP <COUNT>
The most times a verifier sandbox is brought up again, where its health
check does not pass, 0 or more.
.TP
\fB\-\-model\fP <NAME>
The name of the model the harnesses call, as its server knows it.
.TP
\fB\-\-model\-key\-file\fP <FILE>
The file that holds the key the model is called with, where its server asks
for one.
.TP
\fB\-\-model\-url\fP <URL>
The address the model is served at.
.TP
\fB\-\-outputs\-dir\fP <DIR>
The outputs directory, which start creates.
.TP
\fB\-\-primogenitor\fP <DIR>
The directory of source code copied to make the primogenitor.
.TP
\fB\-\-selection\-tasks\-percentage\fP <NUMBER>
The percentage of each benchmark's tasks that are selection tasks, above 0 and
below 100.
.SH THE IMAGE OF THE DIRECTED EVOLUTION
Every diversification, and every build of a harness, is carried out in a
sandbox of one image, the image of the directed evolution.
The diversification of an experiment is carried out in a sandbox of that
image as well, and no trial is: a trial is carried out in the environment of
its task.
The image of the directed evolution is the image that
\fB\-\-diversification\-docker\-image\fP names, or the image that cladenc
builds when the directed evolution is started, from the Dockerfile that
\fB\-\-diversification\-dockerfile\fP names, as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
timeout \-s KILL <SECONDS> docker build \-\-tag <NAME> \-\-file <FILE> <DIR>
.EE
.RE
.TP 11n
\fB<SECONDS>\fP
the value of \fB\-\-diversification\-docker\-image\-build\-timeout\fP, and
five seconds more
.TP 11n
\fB<NAME>\fP
the name cladenc gives the image: cladenc\- and sixteen random hexadecimal
digits
.TP 11n
\fB<FILE>\fP
a copy of the Dockerfile, kept in image in the outputs directory
.TP 11n
\fB<DIR>\fP
an empty directory, so the Dockerfile has no file to copy into the image
.PP
The build has the time limit that
\fB\-\-diversification\-docker\-image\-build\-timeout\fP gives, and image in
the outputs directory keeps what Docker printed.
cladenc ends a build that runs past its time limit, and the build ends itself
five seconds later where cladenc was killed outright, as by \fBkill \-9\fP,
and is not there to end it.
The machine cladenc runs on has to hold timeout, which the package coreutils
brings.
An image that is not built stops \fBcladenc start\fP with exit status 2,
before the directed evolution has a journal.
The image stays on the machine when the directed evolution ends: \fBcladenc
resume\fP carries out its diversifications and builds in it, and does not
build it again.
.PP
cladenc fetches no image, and has no flag for a registry, the server that
Docker fetches an image from.
The name of an image says which registry it is of, as
registry.example:5000/tools/harness:1 does, and a name that says none is of
Docker Hub.
The image that \fB\-\-diversification\-docker\-image\fP names has to be on the
machine before the directed evolution: fetched there with \fBdocker pull\fP,
carried there as a file with \fBdocker save\fP and \fBdocker load\fP, or built
there.
cladenc tells Docker never to fetch the image of a sandbox, so a directed
evolution whose image is not on the machine stops at the first sandbox of
that image, and \fBcladenc resume\fP continues it once the image is there.
A Dockerfile whose base image is on the machine, and whose steps fetch
nothing, is built with no connection to the internet.
cladenc does not ask Docker to fetch a base image and cannot forbid it: Docker
fetches a base image that is not on the machine, from the registry of its
name.
.SH ENVIRONMENTS
cladenc builds the environment of every task of every benchmark when the
directed evolution is started, and again each time it is resumed, before it
brings up any sandbox, as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
docker build \-\-tag <NAME> \-\-label cladenc.task=<TASK> \e
    \-\-label cladenc.directed\-evolution=<PATH> <DIR>
.EE
.RE
.TP 8n
\fB<NAME>\fP
the name cladenc gives the environment: cladenc\-environments\- and sixteen
random hexadecimal digits, the same for every task of the directed evolution,
then a colon and the place of the task among the tasks of the directed
evolution, counted from 0
.TP 8n
\fB<TASK>\fP
the task, as <BENCHMARK>/<TASK>
.TP 8n
\fB<PATH>\fP
the path of the outputs directory, with every symbolic link in it resolved
.TP 8n
\fB<DIR>\fP
the environment directory of the copy of the task in the outputs directory,
the build context of its Dockerfile
.PP
The two labels are Docker labels, each a name and a value that Docker keeps
with an image.
The second is the label that every sandbox of the directed evolution
carries.
It makes the environments of two directed evolutions two images, even where
both are built from one task of one benchmark, so that removing those of one
leaves those of the other on the machine.
.PP
The build has the time limit that the task.toml of the task gives, as
build_timeout_sec under environment, or 600 seconds where it gives none.
environments/<BENCHMARK>/<TASK> in the outputs directory keeps what Docker
printed.
A task whose environment is not built stops the directed evolution with exit
status 2, and \fBcladenc start\fP leaves it no journal.
A resumed directed evolution builds each environment again, under the same
name.
The image that the Dockerfile of a task starts from has to be on the machine:
cladenc does not ask Docker to fetch it and cannot forbid it, so Docker
fetches a base image that is not on the machine, from the registry of its
name.
.PP
cladenc removes the environments it built once \fBcladenc start\fP or
\fBcladenc resume\fP ends, whether the directed evolution finished, stopped or
could not go on, as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
docker image rm \-\-force <NAME>...
.EE
.RE
.PP
so no directed evolution leaves them on the machine.
An image that the task.toml of a task names is not one cladenc built, and is
left alone.
A cladenc that was killed outright removes nothing: \fBcladenc resume\fP
builds the environments again, and removes them when it ends.
.PP
A stop while the image of the directed evolution or an environment is built
comes before the directed evolution has a journal.
\fBcladenc start\fP then exits with status 2 and says that the directed
evolution was stopped before it was started: \fBcladenc resume\fP has nothing
to continue, and the outputs directory is removed by hand before it is named
to \fBcladenc start\fP again.
.PP
A task whose task.toml names an image, as docker_image under environment, has
that image as its environment: cladenc builds nothing for the task, and the
task needs no environment directory.
The image has to be on the machine before the directed evolution, as the image
that \fB\-\-diversification\-docker\-image\fP names has to be.
cladenc looks for it when the directed evolution is started, and again each
time it is resumed, as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
docker image inspect \-\-format {{.Id}} <IMAGE>
.EE
.RE
.PP
and a task whose image is not on the machine stops the directed evolution with
exit status 2.
\fBcladenc start\fP then leaves it no journal.
.SH HARNESSES
A harness is started in a sandbox, of the image of the directed evolution
for a diversification and of the environment of its task for a trial, as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sh \-c <TIMER> <COMMAND> <SECONDS>
.EE
.RE
.TP 11n
\fB<TIMER>\fP
sh \-c "$0" & run=$!; (sleep "$1"; kill \-9 $run) > /dev/null 2>&1 & wait $run
.TP 11n
\fB<COMMAND>\fP
the value of \fB\-\-harness\-run\-command\fP
.TP 11n
\fB<SECONDS>\fP
what is left of the time limit of the command when it is started, in whole
seconds, and five seconds more
.PP
The timer is a line of shell that starts the command, ends it once the seconds
have passed, and hands on its exit status.
Every sandbox of a directed evolution is started under it, or, for a
trial, under sleep, so that a sandbox ends itself once its time limit has
passed, also when cladenc was killed outright, as by \fBkill \-9\fP, and is
not there to remove it.
The image of the directed evolution has to hold sh and sleep, and the
environment of every task sh, sleep, rm, mkdir, chown and ln.
.PP
Until then a sandbox that was left goes on.
\fBcladenc resume\fP removes the sandboxes that the directed evolution left
running before it does anything else, and \fBcladenc\-resume\fP(1) says how
they are found, and how they are removed by hand where the directed evolution
is not resumed.
.PP
In the sandbox of a harness,
.TP 7n
\fB/var/opt/cladenc/harness\fP
holds the source code of the harness, a copy made for this sandbox
.TP 7n
\fB/var/opt/cladenc/work\fP
in a diversification, the clone that the harness diversifies, and the working
directory
.TP 21n
\fBCLADENC_HARNESS\fP
/var/opt/cladenc/harness
.TP 21n
\fBCLADENC_INSTRUCTION\fP
what the harness is asked to do: in a diversification the diversification
prompt, and in a trial the instruction of its task
.TP 21n
\fBCLADENC_MODEL\fP
the name of the model, the value of \fB\-\-model\fP
.TP 21n
\fBCLADENC_MODEL_URL\fP
the address the model is served at, the value of \fB\-\-model\-url\fP
.TP 21n
\fBCLADENC_MODEL_KEY\fP
the key the model is called with, read from the file that
\fB\-\-model\-key\-file\fP names, and not set where that flag is not given
.PP
In a diversification the command runs as the user cladenc runs as, and in a
trial as the user of the environment of the task, or as the user that the
task.toml of the task names.
It is given nothing on standard input, and its standard output and standard
error are kept.
.SH THE MODEL
cladenc calls no model.
A harness calls the model itself, at the address it is told.
In every sandbox, host.docker.internal names the machine cladenc runs on, so
a model served on that machine at port 11434 is named by
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
\-\-model\-url http://host.docker.internal:11434
.EE
.RE
.PP
and is reached as long as its server listens on an address a sandbox can
reach.
The key is read from its file when a directed evolution is started, and again
each time the directed evolution is resumed.
cladenc keeps the key off every command line, out of the journal and out of
what it prints.
.SH DIVERSIFICATIONS
A new harness is made in two steps, a cloning and a diversification.
In the cloning, cladenc copies the source code of a harness, the progenitor,
as it is kept under harnesses in the outputs directory: the copy is the
progenitor's clone.
In the diversification, the progenitor is started in a sandbox, where
/var/opt/cladenc/work holds its clone, and the diversification prompt asks the
progenitor to improve the clone: what the progenitor leaves there is its child.
The diversification prompt is a text that cladenc holds itself, the same in
every diversification: it says what the harness is asked to do, and what the
harness is given to read.
No benchmark brings the diversification prompt: the text that a task brings is
the instruction of the task, which a harness is given in a trial.
In a diversification, CLADENC_INSTRUCTION holds the diversification prompt.
/var/log/cladenc/trials holds the finished trials of the progenitor on the
non-holdout tasks, to read: one directory for each benchmark, and in it one
for each task, holding what the trial on that task kept.
.PP
What this section says of a diversification, it says of one in which a
progenitor diversifies its own clone.
The diversification of an experiment, in which the primogenitor diversifies a
second clone of the progenitor, is said under EXPERIMENTS.
.PP
A progenitor is given more to read, and nothing more to change: what was kept
of each harness it descends from, back to the primogenitor, and of each
harness it made in an earlier diversification that has ended, whether that one
was created or not.
It is given nothing of any other harness.
.TP 7n
\fB/var/log/cladenc/line/<ID>\fP
a harness it descends from
.TP 7n
\fB/var/log/cladenc/children/<ID>\fP
a harness it made before
.PP
Each of these directories is named by the id of its harness, and holds
.TP 17n
\fBharness\fP
the source code of that harness
.TP 17n
\fBtrials\fP
its trials on the non-holdout tasks, laid out as /var/log/cladenc/trials
is
.TP 17n
\fBdiversification\fP
what the harness that made it printed, in stdout and stderr, with the id of
that harness in the file named progenitor and one word in the file named
outcome: created, unchanged, failed, build_failed or timed_out
.TP 17n
\fBbuild\fP
what its build printed, in stdout and stderr
.PP
A directory that would hold nothing is not there: the primogenitor has no
diversification, a harness that was not created has no trials, and a harness
that was not built has no build.
Nothing of a holdout task is in any of them.
.PP
The diversification prompt asks the progenitor for a change to how its child
works on any task: one that answers something it saw in several of its trials,
not in one, and that leaves in the source code nothing that names a task, a
file of a task or the answer to one.
It says that taking out what does not help is an improvement as well, that the
child may be asked in its turn to improve a clone of its own, so that making
the child better at that counts, and that the child is judged by tasks the
progenitor is not shown.
The diversification prompt is a request: cladenc enforces none of it.
.PP
A harness is cloned only once it has been benchmarked in full, on every task
of every benchmark of the directed evolution, so that it finds every
non-holdout task in /var/log/cladenc/trials.
The primogenitor is benchmarked in full before the first diversification.
.PP
Every harness is benchmarked in full once it is created, whether it is cloned
or not.
cladenc chooses which harness is cloned, partly by chance.
It does not choose which trial starts next: the created harness with the
lowest id that has a task left starts a trial on it.
.PP
\fB\-\-max\-diversifications\fP gives the most diversifications a directed
evolution starts.
A diversification counts once it is started, whether or not it creates its
child.
The diversification of an experiment is not counted.
The directed evolution ends once that many have been started, each of them has
ended, and every created harness has been benchmarked in full.
With one benchmark of 5 tasks and \fB\-\-max\-diversifications\fP 1, the
primogenitor is benchmarked on the 5 tasks, is cloned and diversifies its
clone, and its child is benchmarked on the 5 tasks where the diversification
created it.
.PP
A diversification starts only once no trial is left to start, no trial is
running and no other diversification is under way: every harness created so
far has been benchmarked in full, the child of the diversification before
among them.
So cladenc chooses each harness that is cloned with what every created
harness completed in hand.
A singleton control group harness is not waited for, since it is never cloned:
the diversification of its experiment and its trials go on beside a
diversification.
While a diversification runs, no sandbox of another child is started, so
fewer sandboxes run than \fB\-\-max\-concurrent\-sandboxes\fP allows.
.PP
How the harness ends decides what is recorded:
.TP 18n
\fBexit status 0\fP
the diversification created a harness, or is recorded as unchanged where the
harness changed nothing
.TP 18n
\fBexit status 125\fP
Docker did not start the sandbox: the directed evolution stops, and
\fBcladenc resume\fP carries out the diversification again
.TP 18n
\fBany other status\fP
the diversification failed
.TP 18n
\fBthe time limit\fP
the sandbox is removed, and the diversification is recorded as timed out
.PP
A diversification in which the progenitor leaves its clone as it was creates
no child, and is recorded as unchanged.
cladenc compares what the progenitor left in /var/opt/cladenc/work with the
source code of the progenitor, as it is kept under harnesses in the outputs
directory.
The two are the same where they hold the same files, directories and symbolic
links, each file with the same contents and the same permission bits, and each
symbolic link with the same target.
Such a child would be its progenitor over again, and a model does not answer
the same way twice, so what the child completed would differ from what its
progenitor completed by luck alone.
It is not built and not benchmarked, and it is never chosen to be cloned.
.PP
A child that holds anything but regular files, directories and symbolic links
is a failed diversification.
The source code of every child is kept under harnesses in the outputs
directory, created or not.
diversifications in the outputs directory keeps, in a directory named after
the child, the standard output and standard error of the harness that made it,
the id of that harness in the file named progenitor, and how the
diversification ended in the file named outcome.
.SH EXPERIMENTS
Where the progenitor is not the primogenitor, cladenc conducts an experiment
to test whether a progenitor is better at diversifying clones into better
harnesses than the primogenitor.
To conduct the experiment, cladenc establishes a baseline: it clones the
progenitor a second time, and has the primogenitor diversify the second clone.
The harness the primogenitor creates by diversifying the second clone of the
progenitor is the singleton control group harness.
cladenc brings up one sandbox more for that diversification, in which
.TP 7n
\fB/var/opt/cladenc/harness\fP
holds a copy of the source code of the primogenitor, which is what runs
.TP 7n
\fB/var/opt/cladenc/work\fP
holds the second clone of the progenitor, which is what is diversified
.PP
The primogenitor is given the diversification prompt that the progenitor is
given, and what the progenitor is given to read: under /var/log/cladenc/trials
the trials of the progenitor on the non-holdout tasks, under
/var/log/cladenc/line the harnesses the progenitor descends from, and under
/var/log/cladenc/children the children the progenitor made before the
progenitor's child of the experiment.
What the primogenitor leaves in /var/opt/cladenc/work is the singleton control
group harness.
Where the primogenitor leaves the second clone as it was, no singleton control
group harness is created, and the diversification is recorded as unchanged.
.PP
The singleton control group harness is built and benchmarked in full, as the
progenitor's child is, and it takes no other part in the directed evolution:
it is never chosen to be cloned, it is counted in no clade, it is not named
the best harness, and the diversification it is created in does not count
toward \fB\-\-max\-diversifications\fP.
It is nobody's child, so no harness that diversifies a clone is given anything
of it.
Where the progenitor is the primogenitor, no experiment is conducted: the
progenitor's child and the singleton control group harness would be created in
the same way.
.PP
The source code of every singleton control group harness is kept under
harnesses in the outputs directory, created or not.
baselines in the outputs directory keeps, in a directory named after the
singleton control group harness, the standard output and standard error of the
primogenitor, the id of the progenitor in the file named progenitor, the id of
the progenitor's child in the file named child, and how the diversification
ended in the file named outcome.
.SH BUILDS
Where \fB\-\-harness\-build\-command\fP is given, a harness is built before it
is run for the first time, in a sandbox of the same image, as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sh \-c <TIMER> <COMMAND> <SECONDS>
.EE
.RE
.PP
where <COMMAND> is the value of that flag and the timer is the same one, with
/var/opt/cladenc/harness as the working directory, which holds the source code
of the harness and is what CLADENC_HARNESS names.
A build calls no model, and is given none of the three variables of the model.
What the build makes stays there, and is part of every copy made of the
harness.
The primogenitor is built before any diversification or trial, within the
time limit of one diversification.
A child is built as the last step of its diversification, within what is left
of the time limit of that diversification.
.PP
builds in the outputs directory keeps the standard output and standard error
of every build, in a directory named after the harness.
.PP
How a build ends decides what is recorded:
.TP 18n
\fBexit status 0\fP
the harness is built
.TP 18n
\fBexit status 125\fP
Docker did not start the sandbox: the directed evolution stops, and
\fBcladenc resume\fP builds again
.TP 18n
\fBany other status\fP
a child is recorded as build_failed, and is not created; for the primogenitor
the directed evolution stops, and \fBcladenc resume\fP builds it again
.TP 18n
\fBthe time limit\fP
the sandbox is removed, and for a child the diversification is recorded as
timed out; for the primogenitor the directed evolution stops
.PP
A build that leaves anything but regular files, directories and symbolic links
has failed.
.SH BENCHMARKS
A benchmark is a directory that holds one directory for each task.
The name of a task's directory is the id of the task, made of letters, digits,
dots, underscores and hyphens, and the directory holds
.TP 16n
\fBinstruction.md\fP
a file of text, the instruction of the task, which says what is to be done
.TP 16n
\fBtask.toml\fP
a file of text, the task configuration of Harbor, which says how a trial on the task is carried
out
.TP 16n
\fBtests\fP
a directory that holds test.sh, an executable file, the verifier of the task,
and whatever else the verifier uses
.TP 16n
\fBenvironment\fP
a directory that holds a file named Dockerfile, which describes the
environment of the task, the image its trials are carried out in with the
files the work starts from, and whatever else the Dockerfile uses
.PP
as in Harbor's task layout, and may hold more, such as solution, which no
sandbox is ever given.
A file that is in the benchmark and in no task is left alone.
A benchmark that holds no task is refused, and so is one with a task that
lacks one of the four, or whose environment directory holds a Compose file,
such as docker\-compose.yaml: an environment of more than one container cannot
be built.
A task whose task.toml names an image needs neither the Dockerfile nor the
environment directory.
.PP
A task whose verifier runs in a sandbox of its own, as said below, is refused
as well for a Compose file in its tests directory.
Where the image of that sandbox holds the verifier, the task needs no test.sh.
.PP
\fB\-\-benchmark\fP is given once for each benchmark of the directed
evolution.
A directed evolution that is given several has each harness benchmarked on all
of them, and looks for the one harness that completes the most of their
selection tasks.
Every selection task counts the same, so a benchmark with more of them weighs
more.
The name of a benchmark is the name of its directory, made of the characters
the id of a task is made of, and a task of a directed evolution is known by
the two together, as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
<BENCHMARK>/<TASK>
.EE
.RE
.PP
so two benchmarks may each hold a task of the same id.
Two benchmarks whose directories have the same name are refused.
The tasks are copied into benchmarks in the outputs directory when a directed
evolution is started, each into a directory named after its benchmark, so the
directed evolution keeps the tasks it started with, and \fBcladenc resume\fP
does not read the directories that \fB\-\-benchmark\fP named.
.SH HOLDOUT TASKS
\fB\-\-selection\-tasks\-percentage\fP gives the part of the tasks of each
benchmark that are selection tasks, as so many in a hundred: a number above 0
and below 100, such as 45 or 12.5.
\fB\-\-corroboration\-tasks\-percentage\fP gives the part that are
corroboration tasks, in the same way.
The selection tasks and the corroboration tasks together are the holdout
tasks.
The holdout tasks are the tasks that no progenitor reads about, and the tasks
that are not held out are the non-holdout tasks.
cladenc draws the selection tasks and the corroboration tasks at random when a
directed evolution is started, out of each benchmark by itself: the number of
its tasks times the percentage, divided by 100 and rounded to the nearest
whole number, a half rounded up.
At 45 and 15, a benchmark of 500 tasks has 225 selection tasks, 75
corroboration tasks and 200 non-holdout tasks.
.PP
A \fB\-\-selection\-tasks\-percentage\fP that leaves a benchmark no selection
task is refused, and so is a \fB\-\-corroboration\-tasks\-percentage\fP that
leaves a benchmark no corroboration task, and so are two values that together
leave a benchmark no non-holdout task.
So a benchmark holds three tasks at the least, and a benchmark of fewer is
refused.
The draw is made once for a directed evolution and written in its journal, and
\fBcladenc resume\fP reads it there and draws nothing.
.PP
Every harness is benchmarked on the holdout tasks as on the non-holdout tasks,
selection tasks and corroboration tasks alike, and nothing a harness is given
in a trial says which of the sets its task is in.
A harness that diversifies a clone is given the trials of the progenitor on
the non-holdout tasks, and nothing of the holdout tasks: no instruction, no
work, no result, and no count of them.
.PP
Artificial selection goes by the selection tasks and by no others.
cladenc chooses which harness is cloned by the selection tasks that the
harness and its descendants completed and did not complete, and it names as
the best harness the created harness, benchmarked in full, that completed the
most selection tasks: of several that completed as many, the one created
first.
The non-holdout tasks are what a harness reads to find what to change, and a
harness that completes more of those alone is not chosen for it.
.PP
cladenc counts for every harness the corroboration tasks it completed, and
chooses nothing by them: not the harness that is cloned, and never the best
harness.
A set of tasks gives a fair count only while nothing is chosen by it.
.PP
The best harness is named among many, each benchmarked once, and a model does
not answer the same way twice.
So the best of many harnesses that are all alike has still completed more
selection tasks than the primogenitor, by luck.
That luck does not reach the corroboration tasks: a harness that was lucky on
the selection tasks is no luckier on other tasks, and a change that makes a
harness complete more tasks shows on both sets.
So how far the best harness is ahead of the primogenitor is read in the
columns of the corroboration tasks of \fBcladenc report\fP.
A lead there corroborates the lead on the selection tasks: it came through a
test that could have refuted it, which is not to say that it is proven.
One of two harnesses that are alike is still ahead on the corroboration tasks
by a few of them, and the fewer the corroboration tasks, the more a few of
them weigh.
.SH REPORT
\fBcladenc report\fP is for a directed evolution that has ended, and
\fBcladenc status\fP is for one that has not ended.
Each refuses the other kind, and says which of the two to call.
When a directed evolution ends, \fBcladenc start\fP prints on standard error
what \fBcladenc report\fP prints, after the line that says that the directed
evolution finished, and prints on standard output the directory of the best
harness and nothing else.
\fBcladenc\-report\fP(1) says how the report is read, and
\fBcladenc\-status\fP(1) how the state so far is read.
.SH WHAT LUCK GIVES
How many more selection tasks the best of many harnesses that are all alike
completes is measured by a directed evolution in which every harness works as
the primogenitor does: one carried out with the values of the directed
evolution it is set beside, and with a run command that, where the harness is
started for a diversification, calls no model and adds one line to a file that
no harness reads, as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
\-\-harness\-run\-command \(aqif [ \-d /var/log/cladenc/trials ]
    then echo >> diversified; else <COMMAND>; fi\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
where <COMMAND> is the run command of the other, and diversified is a name
that no file of the primogenitor has.
/var/log/cladenc/trials is there for a diversification and not for a trial,
so every diversification calls no model, and creates a child that is the
source code of its progenitor with one line more in the file named
diversified.
The line is added because a diversification that changes nothing creates no
child.
Such a directed evolution holds as many children as
\fB\-\-max\-diversifications\fP gives, each of them benchmarked in full.
In \fBcladenc report\fP of it, how far apart the counts of the harnesses lie
is what luck does to the count of one harness, and how many more selection
tasks the best harness completed than the primogenitor is what luck and the
choosing give together, with nothing changed in how a harness works.
The best harness of the other directed evolution is ahead of its primogenitor
on the selection tasks by a change only as far as it is ahead by more than
that.
\fBcladenc report\fP also says how many tasks at least one harness completed,
of the selection tasks, of the corroboration tasks and of the non-holdout
tasks, and \fBcladenc status\fP says how many so far.
Where every harness works as the primogenitor does, that is what one more
trial on each task for each child gives the primogenitor, and it is set
beside the count of the best harness of the other directed evolution, which
had one trial on each task.
.SH TRIALS
A trial is one harness working on one task, once, and cladenc carries it
out in two steps, in one sandbox of the environment of the task, started as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sleep <SECONDS>
.EE
.RE
.TP 11n
\fB<SECONDS>\fP
the time limit of the harness and the time limit of the verifier together, the
longest the health check of the task can take, and ten minutes more, for what
cladenc copies in and out
.PP
First the source code of the harness is copied into that sandbox, at
/var/opt/cladenc/harness, and the harness is started there, as it is for a
diversification, in the working directory of the environment and as its user:
the user and the working directory its Dockerfile names, and root and / where
it names none.
CLADENC_INSTRUCTION holds the instruction of the task.
The harness is given nothing else of the task, so it can neither read nor
change the verifier.
Before the harness is started, cladenc makes
.TP 17n
\fB/logs\fP
a symbolic link to /var/log/cladenc/harbor
.TP 17n
\fB/logs/artifacts\fP
an empty directory, for what the harness saves to be kept with the trial
.PP
When the harness has ended, with whatever exit status, the tests directory of
the task is copied into the same sandbox, and its verifier is run there, in
the same working directory and as the same user, unless the task.toml of the
task names a user for the verifier, as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sh \-c <TIMER> /tests/test.sh <SECONDS>
.EE
.RE
.TP 16n
\fB/tests\fP
a symbolic link to /var/opt/cladenc/harbor/tests, which holds a copy of the
tests directory of the task, and nothing that the harness left there
.TP 16n
\fB/logs/verifier\fP
an empty directory, for the verdict of the verifier
.PP
The verifier finds the sandbox as the harness left it: the files the harness
left outside /tests and /logs/verifier, and the processes it left running, are
there as well.
A verifier calls no model, and is given none of the three variables of the
model.
The sandbox is removed once the trial has ended.
.PP
/logs and /tests are the two names that Harbor's task layout fixes at the top
of the filesystem.
cladenc makes no directory there, as the Filesystem Hierarchy Standard asks of
an application: each of the two is a symbolic link.
/logs is a link to /var/log/cladenc/harbor, since /var/log/cladenc holds the
records cladenc keeps, and /tests is a link to /var/opt/cladenc/harbor/tests,
since /var/opt/cladenc holds what cladenc gives a sandbox to work with.
So /logs/artifacts is /var/log/cladenc/harbor/artifacts, and /logs/verifier is
/var/log/cladenc/harbor/verifier.
While the harness runs, the sandbox holds neither /tests nor the directory
that /tests is a link to.
Where the image of the environment already holds something at /logs, cladenc
makes no link and leaves what is there, and makes /logs/artifacts and
/logs/verifier inside it.
What the image or the harness left at /tests is removed before the link is
made.
.SH TIME LIMITS
The harness has the time limit that the task.toml of the task gives, as
timeout_sec under agent, and no time limit where it gives none, as in Harbor.
A harness that runs past its time limit is stopped, and every process it left
running is ended, as by \fBkill \-9 \-1\fP.
What it left is judged all the same.
The verifier has a time limit of its own, timeout_sec under verifier, or 600
seconds where the task gives none, counted from the moment the harness ended
or was stopped.
No flag gives a time limit of a trial.
Where a task gives its harness no time limit, the sandbox of a trial does
not end itself: a cladenc that was killed outright leaves it running until
\fBcladenc resume\fP removes it, or a person does.
.SH HEALTH CHECKS
Where the task.toml of a task gives a health check, as command under
environment.healthcheck, cladenc runs that command in the sandbox before it
copies the source code of the harness in, under the same timer, as the user of
the environment and in its working directory.
The harness is started only once the command has exited with status 0.
A run of the command has the time limit timeout_sec, or 30 seconds where the
task gives none.
A run that exits with another status, or runs past its time limit, has failed,
and the command is run again after interval_sec, or 5 seconds where the task
gives none.
The health check has not passed once the command has failed as many times as
retries says, or 3 times where the task gives none.
A failure within start_period_sec of the first run, or 0 seconds where the
task gives none, is not counted, and the command is then run again after
start_interval_sec, or 5 seconds where the task gives none.
.PP
Where the health check of a task does not pass, the harness was never started,
so cladenc does not record that the harness did not complete the task.
It removes the sandbox, waits, brings up a new sandbox of the task and
runs the health check there again, as many times as
\fB\-\-max\-task\-health\-check\-retries\fP gives.
Before the first retry it waits interval_sec of the health check, or 5 seconds
where the task gives none, and before each retry after that twice as long as
before the last: 5 seconds, then 10, then 20.
It says each retry on standard error.
Where the health check passes in no sandbox, the directed evolution stops
with exit status 1, and no result is recorded for the trial.
cladenc then names the harness and the task, says that the health check of the
task did not pass and in how many tries, and names the directory that holds
what the health check printed the last time it ran.
That directory is under running in the outputs directory, and stays there
until \fBcladenc resume\fP carries out the trial again.
.SH ARTIFACTS
Where the task.toml of a task lists paths under artifacts, cladenc keeps each
with the trial, a file or a directory with all it holds, as the harness left
it: it copies them out of the sandbox once the harness has ended or was
stopped, before the tests directory is copied in.
An entry of the list is a path in the sandbox, or a table that names the
path as source.
The path is kept in artifacts in the directory of the trial, at the place
the table gives as destination, and at the path itself without its leading /
where the table gives none or the entry is a path alone: /app/out/report.json
is kept as artifacts/app/out/report.json.
A symbolic link at the path is followed.
A path that is not in the sandbox keeps nothing, and the trial goes on.
Where two entries would be kept at the same place, or one inside the other,
the first is kept and the other is not.
.PP
Of a directory, what the table lists as exclude is left out.
Each is a pattern as \fBtar \-\-exclude\fP takes one: it leaves out a file or
a directory whose name it matches, with all the directory holds, and a pattern
with a / in it is matched against the path of the file from the directory that
is kept, or from any directory inside it, so that logs/run.txt leaves out
run.txt in every directory named logs.
In a pattern, * stands for any run of characters, / among them, ? for any one
character, the characters between [ and ] for any one of them, and a character
after \e for itself.
.PP
What a harness left in /logs/artifacts is kept as a path that the task lists
is, as artifacts/logs/artifacts, though the task lists no such path, as in
Harbor, and nothing is kept where the harness left nothing there.
cladenc makes the directory before the harness is started, as said above, and
it belongs to the user the harness runs as, so that a harness that does not
run as root saves a file there as well.
A task that lists /logs/artifacts itself, or a path that is kept at the same
place, has that entry obeyed in its place.
.PP
Where the task.toml of a task lists commands under verifier.collect, which
Harbor calls collection hooks, cladenc runs each in the sandbox of the
harness once the harness has ended or was stopped, before it keeps any path,
in the order the task lists them.
A command is run as the harness is, under the same timer and in the working
directory of the environment, as the user its entry names as user, or the user
of the environment where it names none.
It has the time limit that its entry gives as timeout_sec, or 60 seconds where
it gives none.
A command that exits with another status than 0, or runs past its time limit,
does not end the trial: the next command is run, and the paths are kept as
they then are.
collection\-hooks in the directory of the trial keeps what each command
printed, in a directory named after the place of the command in the list,
counted from 1.
.SH VERIFIERS IN A SANDBOX OF THEIR OWN
Where the task.toml of a task says that its verifier runs separately, cladenc
runs the verifier in a sandbox of its own, which holds nothing of the
sandbox of the harness but the paths the task lists under artifacts.
A task says so with environment_mode under verifier, written as separate, or
with a table named environment under verifier, which says so by being there.
A task that gives neither, or writes environment_mode as shared, has its
verifier run in the sandbox of the harness, as said above.
.PP
The verifier sandbox is a sandbox of the first of these that the task
gives:
.TP 4n
\fB1.\fP
the image that docker_image under verifier.environment names, which has to be
on the machine, as an image that a task names for its environment has to be
.TP 4n
\fB2.\fP
an image that cladenc builds from the Dockerfile in the tests directory of the
task, with that directory as the build context
.TP 4n
\fB3.\fP
the environment of the task, as it is before a harness works in it
.PP
An image of 1 or 2 holds the verifier, at /tests/test.sh: cladenc copies no
tests directory into its sandbox and makes nothing at /tests, and the task
needs no test.sh.
Into a sandbox of 3 the tests directory of the task is copied, and /tests is
a symbolic link to the copy, as said above.
cladenc builds an image of 2 when it builds the environments of the tasks, and
as it builds those, under the name of the environment of the task with
\-verifier after it, and removes it when it removes them.
environments/<BENCHMARK>/<TASK>/verifier in the outputs directory keeps what
Docker printed.
An image of 1 that is not on the machine, and an image of 2 that is not built,
stop the directed evolution with exit status 2.
.PP
Where the task.toml holds the table environment under verifier, the verifier
sandbox follows that table and nothing of environment: workdir, env,
cpus, memory_mb, healthcheck and build_timeout_sec are obeyed there as they
are under environment, and a setting the table leaves out is left out.
Where the task.toml holds no such table, the verifier sandbox follows
environment.
What is refused under environment is refused under verifier.environment as
well.
.PP
Once the harness has ended or was stopped, and the paths under artifacts are
kept, cladenc removes the sandbox of the harness and brings up that of the
verifier, so that a trial is one sandbox at a time.
Where the settings of the verifier give a health check, cladenc runs it there as
it runs that of the task, and nothing else is done in the sandbox until the
health check has passed.
/logs is then made the symbolic link that it is in the sandbox of the
harness, and each path that was kept is copied to the same path in the
verifier sandbox, the path of its entry and not destination, without
what exclude left out.
A directory takes the place of what the image holds at that path.
.PP
Where the health check of the verifier sandbox does not pass, no verifier
has judged what the harness left, so cladenc does not record that the harness
did not complete the task.
It removes the sandbox, waits, brings up a new verifier sandbox and
runs the health check there again, as many times as
\fB\-\-max\-verifier\-health\-check\-retries\fP gives.
Before the first retry it waits interval_sec of the health check, or 5 seconds
where the task gives none, and before each retry after that twice as long as
before the last: 5 seconds, then 10, then 20.
It says each retry on standard error.
Where the health check passes in no sandbox, the directed evolution stops
with exit status 1, and no result is recorded for the trial.
cladenc then names the harness and the task, says that the health check of the
verifier sandbox did not pass and in how many tries, and names the
directory that holds what the health check printed the last time it ran.
That directory is under running in the outputs directory, and stays there
until \fBcladenc resume\fP carries out the trial again.
.PP
What is copied in is not trusted.
No file keeps a setuid or a setgid bit.
A file that is not a regular file, a directory or a symbolic link is left out,
and so is a symbolic link that points outside the path it was kept with.
What is copied in belongs to the user the verifier runs as.
.PP
Then the tests directory is copied in where the image holds no verifier,
/logs/verifier is emptied, and the verifier is run as said above, in the working
directory and as the user of its own sandbox, unless the task.toml names a
user for the verifier.
Its time limit is counted from then.
The verifier sandbox is started as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sleep <SECONDS>
.EE
.RE
.TP 11n
\fB<SECONDS>\fP
the time limit of the verifier, the longest the health check of the verifier can
take, and ten minutes more
.PP
and is removed once the verifier has ended.
A verifier image has to hold what the environment of a task has to: sh,
sleep, rm, mkdir, chown and ln.
.SH CPUS AND MEMORY
The sandbox of a trial is given no more CPUs and no more memory than its
task asks for, as \fB\-\-cpus\fP and \fB\-\-memory\fP of \fBdocker run\fP, so
the harness and the verifier have them between them.
A task that asks for neither is given what Docker gives any container.
The older spelling of the memory, memory under environment, a number and one
of G, M and K, such as 4G, is read as Harbor reads it, and a task that gives
both spellings with sizes that differ is refused.
cladenc asks Docker what the machine has, as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
docker info \-\-format \(aq{{.NCPU}} {{.MemTotal}}\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
and a task that asks for more CPUs or more memory than that stops the directed
evolution with exit status 2, when it is started and each time it is resumed,
with the task named.
.SH TASK CONFIGURATION
The task.toml of a task is the task configuration of Harbor.
cladenc obeys what it says of the environment of the task:
.TP 7n
\fBdocker_image under environment\fP
the image that is the environment, in place of one built from the Dockerfile
.TP 7n
\fBworkdir under environment\fP
the working directory of the harness and of the verifier, in place of the one
the image names
.TP 7n
\fBenv under environment\fP
variables that the sandbox is given, so the harness and the verifier alike
.TP 7n
\fBuser under agent\fP
the user the harness runs as, a name or a number, in place of the user the
image names
.TP 7n
\fBuser under verifier\fP
the user the verifier runs as, in the same way
.TP 7n
\fBenv under verifier\fP
variables that the verifier alone is given
.TP 7n
\fBtimeout_sec under agent\fP
the time limit of the harness, in seconds
.TP 7n
\fBtimeout_sec under verifier\fP
the time limit of the verifier, in seconds
.TP 7n
\fBenvironment_mode under verifier\fP
separate where the verifier runs in a sandbox of its own, and shared where it
runs in that of the harness
.TP 7n
\fBenvironment under verifier\fP
what environment says of the sandbox of the harness, said of the verifier
sandbox
.TP 7n
\fBcollect under verifier\fP
commands that are run in the sandbox once the harness has ended, before the
paths under artifacts are kept
.TP 7n
\fBbuild_timeout_sec under environment\fP
the time limit of the build of the environment, in seconds
.TP 7n
\fBcpus under environment\fP
the most CPUs the sandbox of a trial is given, a whole number or a part
of one
.TP 7n
\fBmemory_mb under environment\fP
the most memory that sandbox is given, in megabytes
.TP 7n
\fBhealthcheck under environment\fP
a command that has to pass before the harness is started, and how often it is
run
.TP 7n
\fBartifacts\fP
paths in the sandbox that are kept with the trial once the harness has
ended
.PP
cladenc obeys nothing else of a task.toml.
A setting that is left out, or written with nothing between its quotation
marks, changes nothing.
The source code of the harness and /logs/artifacts belong to the user the
harness runs as, and the copy of the tests directory and /logs/verifier belong
to the user the verifier runs as.
.PP
In the value of a variable, ${NAME} stands for the value that the variable
NAME has where cladenc runs, and ${NAME:\-TEXT} for that value, or for TEXT
where NAME is not set there.
A variable that cladenc sets for a harness, such as CLADENC_MODEL, keeps the
value cladenc gives it.
cladenc reads the values when the directed evolution is started, and again
each time it is resumed, and writes none of them in the journal.
It keeps them off every command line: Docker reads them from its standard
input, one line for each variable, so the name of a variable is made of
letters, digits, dots, underscores and hyphens, and its value is one line of
text.
.PP
A task is refused when the directed evolution is started where it holds no
task.toml, or where its task.toml is not valid as the task configuration of
Harbor.
A task.toml is not valid where it is not TOML, where it gives a setting a
value of another kind than the setting takes, or a value that is not one of
those the setting takes, where a table lacks a key it has to hold, such as
command under environment.healthcheck, or where it says under verifier that
the verifier shares the environment of the harness, as environment_mode, and
gives the verifier an environment of its own, as environment.
cladenc checks every setting that Harbor's documentation of the task
configuration names, those it does not obey among them, and lets through a
setting that the documentation does not name.
.PP
A task is also refused where its task.toml names a variable otherwise, or
takes a value from a variable that is not set where cladenc runs and gives no
text in its place.
A time limit that is not a number of seconds above 0 is refused in the same
way, and so are CPUs or megabytes of memory that are not a number above 0.
Of a health check, timeout_sec is refused where it is not a number of seconds
above 0, retries where they are not a number above 0, and interval_sec,
start_period_sec and start_interval_sec where they are not a number of
seconds, 0 or more.
Of an entry under artifacts, the path is refused where it holds .., and
destination where it begins with /, holds .., or names no file or directory.
.PP
A task is refused as well where its task.toml asks for what cladenc has no way
to give: several steps, as [[steps]]; a GPU, as gpus under environment above
0, or a TPU, as tpu; Windows, as os; MCP servers, as mcp_servers; a skills
directory, as skills_dir; a path or a command of a container other than that
of the harness, as service of an entry under artifacts or under
verifier.collect that is not main; or the whole sandbox of the harness in
the verifier sandbox, as an entry under artifacts whose path names no
file or directory, where the verifier runs separately.
The refusal names the task and what it asks for.
.SH RESULTS
The verifier writes its verdict in /logs/verifier/reward.txt, a single number,
or in /logs/verifier/reward.json, an object of labelled numbers, which is read
in place of reward.txt where both are there.
/tests and /logs/verifier are the paths of Harbor's task layout, which a
verifier expects.
.PP
How a trial ends decides its result:
.TP 7n
\fBa verdict of 1, or 1 for every label of reward.json\fP
the harness completed the task
.TP 7n
\fBany other verdict, a file that cannot be read, or no verdict\fP
the harness did not complete the task
.TP 7n
\fBa health check of the task, or of the verifier sandbox, that passes in none of its tries\fP
no result is recorded: the directed evolution stops, and \fBcladenc resume\fP
carries out the trial again
.TP 7n
\fBexit status 125 of either step or of the health check\fP
Docker did not start the sandbox: the directed evolution stops, and
\fBcladenc resume\fP carries out the trial again
.TP 7n
\fBthe time limit of the verifier\fP
the sandbox is removed, and the harness did not complete the task, which is
recorded as timed_out
.PP
The exit status of the verifier decides nothing more.
.SH CAVEATS
A task that is recorded as completed means that the harness did the work only
as far as three things hold, and cladenc sees to none of them.
.PP
A harness reaches the network.
Every sandbox is started on the usual network of Docker, and reaches the
machine cladenc runs on and whatever that machine reaches.
Where a task was made from something public, its answer may be one fetch away,
and a harness can leave something outside its sandbox for a later harness to
find, which holding tasks out does not guard against.
To keep every harness from reaching further than the model, carry out the
directed evolution on a machine that has no other connection, with the model
served on that machine.
.PP
A verifier may trust what the harness left.
The verifier is started in the sandbox as the harness left it, and everything
there was the harness's to change: tests, build files, any program the verifier
starts from the working directory, and the processes the harness left running.
A verifier that runs the tests it finds in the working directory is passed by a
harness that changed those tests, on a holdout task as on any other.
The tests directory of the task is never given to a harness: a verifier that
keeps what it relies on there, and takes nothing from the working directory on
trust, can be passed that way only by a harness that left a process running to
change the copy of that directory, or the verdict, once they are made.
.PP
The model may know the task.
A task taken from a public source may have been among what the model was
trained on.
A harness then completes it from what the model remembers, and a child that
completes more of such tasks may only be better at drawing that out.
.SH KEPT TRIALS
trials in the outputs directory keeps every trial on a task that is not
held out, in a directory named after the harness, in it one named after the
benchmark, and in that one named after the task, which holds
.TP 18n
\fBresult\fP
one word: completed, not_completed or timed_out
.TP 18n
\fBending\fP
one word: ended, or stopped where the harness ran past its time limit
.TP 18n
\fBinstruction\fP
the instruction of the task
.TP 18n
\fBwork\fP
what the harness left in the working directory of the environment, not kept
where that directory is /
.TP 18n
\fBchanges\fP
every path the harness added, changed or removed in the sandbox, one to a
line after A, C or D
.TP 18n
\fBartifacts\fP
what the harness left at the paths the task lists under artifacts, where it
lists any, and in /logs/artifacts
.TP 18n
\fBcollection\-hooks\fP
what each command under verifier.collect printed, in a directory named after
its place in the list, where the task lists any
.TP 18n
\fBstdout\fP
the standard output of the harness
.TP 18n
\fBstderr\fP
the standard error of the harness
.TP 18n
\fBverifier\fP
the standard output and the standard error of the verifier, in stdout and
stderr, and in logs/verifier what the verifier left in /logs/verifier, its
verdict among them; where the verifier ran in a sandbox of its own with a health
check, what the last run of that printed, in health\-check
.TP 18n
\fBhealth\-check\fP
the standard output and the standard error of the last run of the health
check, in stdout and stderr, where the task gives one
.PP
holdout in the outputs directory keeps the trials on the holdout tasks,
selection tasks and corroboration tasks together, in the same way, apart from
the others, so that the directory a harness that diversifies a clone is given
holds none of them.
